The README's git-notes push/fetch caveat got its own prominent section instead of a passing mention because it's the failure mode most likely to blindside someone silently: notes live on refs/notes/commits, a normal clone/fetch/push drops them with no error, and a collaborator just sees no note where one should be -- nothing tells them the ref itself is missing. Burying that in prose risks it being skimmed past exactly when it would matter (first clone onto a second machine).

Scripts were moved from the repo root into scripts/ because SKILL.md already documented scripts/git-why.sh and scripts/git-notes-grep.sh as the paths to invoke, while the actual files lived at the repo root -- following SKILL.md literally on a fresh install would have hit 'no such file' on the very first real use of either lookup. Moving the files to match was simpler and lower-risk than rewriting SKILL.md's already-correct usage section.

The frontmatter name (commit-reasoning-notes) was changed to git-why to match the repo name and the install path (.claude/skills/git-why/). Leaving the mismatch meant the same skill could be referred to by three different strings depending on where you looked -- repo name, install folder, frontmatter identity -- which is confusing to debug and risks tooling that keys off frontmatter name disagreeing with the folder it's actually installed under.

This commit is also the first to track SKILL.md and the two scripts (they existed only as untracked working-tree files before). Design reasoning already embedded in that content, worth recording here since this is its first commit:

Two complementary scripts rather than one: 'I know the file/line' (blame-first, git-why.sh) and 'I remember a concept but not where it lives' (grep-first, git-notes-grep.sh) are different starting points for the same underlying question, and folding both into one script behind flags would make the common case (just pass a file) fussier for no real benefit.

git notes was chosen as the storage mechanism over a sidecar file (e.g. a JSON/markdown log keyed by commit or path) because it's content-addressed to the exact commit automatically, survives history rewrites the way a path-keyed file wouldn't, and needs no separate index to stay in sync with the commit graph -- git already provides the addressing scheme for free.

Method/class-level scoping (attaching reasoning to a specific function rather than the whole commit) was deliberately left out of v1: git notes attach at the commit level, not a line range, so finer scoping would mean inventing and maintaining a separate addressing scheme on top of git's own primitives. Commit-level granularity was judged sufficient for the 'why was this built this way' question the skill targets, and simpler to keep accurate.
